ch is next to next to Hong  Kong actually next include on but I did  I did go back to who went on to do it to  do research so I so I took a multi  method approach so I did a couple of  things first I went to and stayed for a  while and interview people in some of  these villages to try to understand how  the process of local development and  land requisition happened especially  interested and land rights and how  farmers and the rural poor can ensure  that they have access to land that's  theirs and if the state decides to  requisition and redevelop it that they  can get good compensation and just  should try to understand the politics of  these village and what  what causes people to mobilize are not  mobilize in in protests so I combined  this two sort of boots on the ground I  guess almost ethnographic approach with  collecting data so I also did survey  research across a number of villages in  China so kind of using this quantitative  data and the qualitative data I was able  to triangulate on an answer for how it  is that the state has been able to  requisition land and implement the  one-child policy without encountering a  widespread protest in all cases okay so  what did you determine how are they able  to do that I'm particularly interested  in the one-child rule and is is that so  in existence today I thought I had read  that it's that it's not but it is no so  so the Chinese family planning policies  have been modified so you know was never  it's called a one-child policy in in the  West but it was first of all in China it  was never a blanket one-child policies  exceptions policies are different in  different provinces and even to a  certain extent different in different  counties and there are exceptions carved  out for people who are I think  minorities or people who in some cases  if you have a daughter as your first  child you might be able to have a second  child especially if you live in a rural  part of China so yeah so the one-child  policy I mean I think is in many ways  maybe an emblem in the west of social  engineering and social control right in  an authoritarian regime like China and I  think in I mean it's it's an interesting  policy because I think and a lot of  urban China it has a lot of political  legitimacy I think a lot of people would  probably have willingly only had one  child anyway and people I think so these  are people and some sense saw the need  for it it's a much less popular policy  in rural China partly for economic  reasons it's just more useful to have  more children or if you're farming or  you need to send children to cities to  work and then remit your wages back home  so it's it's a much less popular in  rural China policy in rural China which  is when I was focusing on so the kind of  puzzle there is why do people why do  people in rural trying to comply with  this anyway and so one answer to this is  is coercion but and about another piece  to this which is the piece that I  explore which I  I think social scientists have thought  about less is the non-coercive side of  it which is the way that the state uses  social organizations to try to influence  them okay and let's talk about that how  do they use those things what are some  of the strategies that they employ to  kind of covertly control the people so I  talked about three different strategies  in my book the first is using local  source social organizations especially  local civil society organizations as a  direct tool of social controls okay tell  us what a civil group is so the sorts of  civil society groups that I look at in  my projects are mostly rural social  organizations so you could think of  temple associations folk religious  organizations that might that have local  temple groups lineage associations these  are kinship networks that might create a  local Association and have an ancestral  hall with ancestral tablets to venerate  and celebrate their ancestors also more  kind of sort of traditional folk music  groups are another kind of social  organization all right so it sounds like  a very die group a diverse group so how  do they tap those organizations to kind  of manipulate what they what they want  to happen so there are a few different  ways and it's not completely I don't  want to paint the picture that is  completely insidious process process a  lot of times where these are  organizations that people of course are  genuinely interested in an interest in  starting so especially so China in the  50s 60s and 70s when Mao Zedong was in  power abolish a lot of these groups and  tried to get rid of them they sort of  reconstituted or revived starting in the  1980s and even though China as a  communist country is sort of nominally  or at least tries to discourage religion  and some of these local civic  organizations however as I think in the  80s when these groups and 90s when these  groups were getting started again local  officials often saw them as useful for  because the existence of this local  group meant two things one if you have  members of these groups it's a little  bit easier to to monitor them and see  what's going on so if you have people  regularly coming and going to one of  these temples or ancestral halls it just  makes them a little bit easier to  monitor I think it's also especially  useful because these groups up and have  leaders who then have social influence  and this informal social influence is  really useful for officials when they  want to implement policy so if you want  to convince somebody and they should  comply with a one-child policy you're  going to convince somebody that they  should move off their land so the  village can build a new factory it's  useful to have these socially  influential allies who can help persuade  you to move people off their lands so I  think it's it's more process of sort of  allowing these groups to to flourish as  they're useful describe how the groups  are actually I'm curious to know like  does the [ __ ] does the government  contact one person in the group and says  hey you know we kind of want it this or  that to happen I mean how does the  process actually work I'm curious the  very nitty-gritty of that they need  agree but so so it's often a kind of  organic an organic process that's that  happens over time so you can have you  you might have in a village the leader  for example of a local lineage group  again these are kind of extended kinship  networks so you can think of as  essentially clans and the leader of this  kinship group might event someone who  sort of emerged over time is just  somebody who was unusually competence  and authoritative mm-hmm someone taking  whom others and the group would go to  sort of ask for advice to try to get a  job to sort of ask what though what's  the right social etiquette for putting  on a wedding you know how many pounds of  rice do I need to bring to the bride and  so you in a lot of villages and you have  somebody who is maybe the kind of  informal or de-facto leader of this of  this kinship network once that person  emerges it's it's can be kind of a kind  of natural process than for local  political leaders like the Communist  Party secretary of the village is sort  of the most powerful political figure in  a village in China for this person to  seek out their socially influential  person and say well listen I need to we  to take  land from the following five farmers who  are in your who are in your clan or  because we need to build a road or  because we need to put in a you know a  power transformer or eminent domain in  this country exactly it's like eminent  domain you have to get people to  actually reply with and especially if  you're not offering compensation at at  market rate we're definitely what  happens or what happens in a lot of  these cases especially in the world  China okay so so it's useful to have  these local elites as social as allies  for the state to help to help implement  these policies and a more kind of direct  oh go ahead and then would it be an  honor for the people who comply with the  request I mean culturally how would they  be looked upon by the rest of the the  group well I think that in general the  reason it's useful to have these allies  is because they do have social Authority  so I'm not sure it's exactly I'm not  sure it's an honor but I think there is  a kind of social obligation that comes  with being a member of these social  groups that means that you need to  listen to the people who have power and  I think this of course this isn't  something that's specific or strange or  unique to to China or to how lineage  groups or temple groups or neighborhood  associations are organized in China I  mean you could think of you know  politically or socially influential  people in your own neighborhood who  might be you know they could be priests  or you know rabbis or just somebody who  has become a kind of neighborhood leader  because they're if they're charismatic  and energetic and there's a certain  amount of social pressure I think to  listen to listen to people in these  positions of social Authority so I'm  sort of looking at what's often the kind  of the flipside of these social groups I  think in academia we often think about  civil society as beneficial to democracy  and I think that there that that can  that can be the case these organizations  can be used you know to mobilize people  to hold political leaders accountable  when they don't behave well the flipside  of that to that though is that these  social groups can also be useful for  political leaders when they want to get  something done that doesn't always  benefit local society mmm-hmm so what  happens when local society doesn't want  to do what something what someone has  suggested that they do  one of these political leaders or the  leader of the civil society so the flip  side to this is that that I argue that  places that don't have strong civil  society groups or don't have strong what  I call infiltrating organizations which  are more direct organizations actually  set up by the Communist Party so party  sells small groups which are sort of the  equivalent of like a neighborhood watch  group which is directly controlled and  set up by the party these are also very  useful direct mechanisms of control  through grassroots organizations the  flip side to this is when these  organizations don't exist it's actually  almost paradoxically easier for social  groups to organize so in the acts sort  of absence of civil society it's easier  to get leaderless protest so this is  something that I saw in a number of  villages to people who were for example  unhappy with their lands being  requisitioned by the state and they  weren't getting very good compensation  they didn't feel like in the long run  this is a good deal for them they wanted  to hold on to the land that had been you  know in their family for in some cases  over a dozen generations so what did  they do they didn't have a local sort of  civil society lineage Association that  would help them organize so instead they  turned to sort of leaderless quasi  leaderless methods of political  organization so such as what so in the  case of one village they a small group  of villagers basically anonymously hung  up posters all over the village saying  okay let's all we're all unhappy they  sort of took the temperature for but he  knew that everybody was unhappy and hung  posters up around the village telling  people okay we're going to be here at at  the village square at a certain time on  a certain day to put pressure on local  leaders and protest and protest against  this protest against his land  requisition and how does that work I  mean what was the outcome so the outcome  was they got to keep their land because  basically enough of the people in the  village mobilized I think something like  eighty percent of the village actually  ended up coming out to the square even  though there wasn't there wasn't a kind  of clear leader this protest was just  some anonymous citizen or group of  citizens who was putting these posters  up around the village enough people came  out that higher levels of local  government to  notice and saw what was going on in this  village and ended up halting halting the  land requisition so people were able to  get what they want by mobilizing but it  was helpful to to use this tactic of  leaderless protest okay so I guess my  next question would be were those people  fearful that there would be any  retribution by the government for coming  out and basically saying that they were  against what was happening well I think  there's power and there's power in  numbers and I think that they would have  legitimately been fearful if there were  a small number of people who are the  clear leaders of this protest and that  does happen where it's there are leaders  of the protests it's possible to  identify who they are and then they face  pressure the pressure could either be  pressure from their family members who  might have jobs government jobs and are  threatened to lose their be threatened  to lose their jobs if they don't  pressure their own family to stand down  or of course they might be jailed or  beaten by the police if there are clear  leaders of the protest if it's a  leaderless there are no leaders that are  going to put in jail and if you have 80  percent of 80 percent of a town coming  out to protest it's the government going  to jail 80 percent of the people in the  town  clearly not so it forces it to certain  extent it can force local governments to  make concessions okay so let's talk  about what's going on in Hong Kong for  the past six months what's your take on  that situation so the dynamics in Hong  Kong are I mean there I think there's  some interesting similarities to what  happens in mainland China of course  they're important differences but the so  the important differences really briefly  Hong Kong has been a sort of it's been a  special administrative region of China  since since 1997 after the handover from  from the British and so Hong Kong has  been guaranteed certain protections  under the one country two systems policy  especially greater civil liberty  liberties then then exist in the  mainland so I think though the protest  was originally motivated by a desire to  try to hold on to these civil liberties  so the Hong Kong government had proposed  an extradition law that people were  worried would that allow the  government in the mainland to then  extradite people who spoke out against  it so that was as those sort of what  drove what drove these protests I think  one of the interesting things is the  tactics used by the protesters and the  tactics used by the government are quite  similar to what happens in the mainland  so on the protesters side one of the  reasons why these protests have  succeeded is precisely they've they've  adopted this leaderless protest model  that I described to you as happens in  the mainland and Hong Kong it's a much  more hi-tech model then going and sort  of putting up posters around the village  they're using encrypted social media  apps right like telegram to to organize  and decide where the protests decide  what tactics to use protesters are also  using online forums to do this and it's  partly partly a reaction against what  happened to an earlier wave of protests  in 2014 when there were when there were  clear leaders of this protest and then  the state the state targeted them as  often happens there's nothing happens in  the mainland so how's the government  responded well so one of the tactics I  mean obviously the most important thing  the government has responded to use  outright coercion a ton of tear gas  rubber bullets and and arrests at the  same time the government's also tried to  use social organisations as a way to  mobilize pro-government Pro mainland  support in a way that I think echoes  what happens with these civil society  groups in the mainland so in Hong Kong  these groups are one type of group that  has on this our native place  associations so these are people who  have moved to Hong Kong or their  ancestors maybe a generation or two  before have moved to Hong Kong from a  place in mainland China like Fujian and  then they set up associations just with  clubs right for for people to organize  and and the government to some extent  has used these associations to mobilize  people against against the pro-democracy  protesters and in support of of the  mainland government so this is a kind of  inversion of what you usually think of  as you have a robust civil society the  civil society is going to strengthen  democracy in this case at least one  elements of Hong Kong civil society is  being used to organize in support of the  government and against these  pro-democracy protesters okay so what do  you  do you think the future hulls for the  protesters what you think will end up  happening that's a good question I mean  there's no way to know there's no way  just wondering if you have any ideas  well I think that the protests in some  ways have been quite have been  tactically innovative so there's been on  the on the negative side there's been  violence and I think you know violence  against people which is which you can't  really excuse and violence targeting  people from the mainland I think there's  also there's also about a lot of  property damage which i think is you  know ethically different I mean I think  stills maybe not something that's  certainly in the West in the tradition  of non-violent protests that people  would would advocate for but I think is  at least distinct from violence against  people but I think the great the great  mass of protesters aren't violent  towards people and aren't are an  extremists and their desire is basically  for to protect the civil liberties that  they have so they've adopted these  innovative tactics that's where  leaderless protests using these apps and  some more sorts of innovative  cooperative tactics on the grounds it  was the kind of model one of the mottos  of the protest is be like water so the  idea said they're protesters kind of  shift around so that the police can't  surround them and kettle them and arrest  them I think though that this the these  sorts of tactical innovations which have  worked to sustain this protest for a  long time so the protests have going up  been going on for four months now I mean  outlasted the 2014 umbrella movement is  outlasted by revolution and he of Egypt  has outlasted the 1989 movements in in  mainland China but strategically it's  not clear to me where these protests are  going and whether or not I think that  the protesters have succeeded in pushing  back and and wringing concessions from  the governments the government withdrew  the extradition law that I talked about  earlier which is the original impetus  from the protesters if they withdrew  that why are they still protesting so  the protesters actually a five have five  demands and so the government has has  essentially met one of them but they did  have kind of very they did it in sort of  slow motion in a way that I think didn't  really satisfy very many people and so  some of the other two  it's like a demand for an inquiry into  police violence which i think is a  demand that Beijing could potentially  could potentially meet if they wanted to  the resignation of the chief executive  you get is something that could  potentially could potentially happen  amnesty for for all of the for the  protesters who've been arrested which i  think is probably politically more  difficult for Beijing and so I think  that the the fact that the government's  hasn't met these other demands and  especially the fact that the amount of  teargas used a matter of rubber bullets  using the number of arrests has  mobilized  I think surprisingly the rest of Hong  Kong to support these young protesters  so the the frontline protesters are a  lot of young people or college aged or  sometimes even younger maybe you know  into their mid-20s the surprising thing  is that this protests despite the  violence maintains high levels of  support among the Hong Kong middle class  we see this in surveys see this in the  results of elections that were held  recently which the pro-democracy camp  won pretty resounding lean a big  reversal for for Beijing so the fact  that they continue to have this support  among a relatively broad swath of the  Hong Kong population means that I think  will probably continue to see some level  of mobilization I think if the  government was continue to make  concessions that might slowly you might  see a slow fading away mm-hmm  interesting so what would you like your  readers to take away from your book so a  couple I think to two main things to  take away from the book I think the  first is that when we think about how  governments control societies we often  think just about outright violence and  coercion or sometimes we think also  about the ways in which governments  generate legitimacy so in the United  States and democracies there's a lot of  theory around how the democratic process  generates legitimacy so people think  that the government's laws and policies  are just and then comply with them I  think that there's a whole range of  tactics the government's use in between  this that are neither quite about  legitimacy and are nor are they violent  coercion that are more sort of hidden  forms of a coercion that get people to  comply with the state  so that's the sort of mean kind of  theoretical takeaway I mean I think also  in the bigger picture I think the book  one of the things that I think I hope  people take away from the book is that I  think it's a it's also a hopeful book  and a lot of ways I think it hopefully  tells the story of a lot of people who  are able to get what they want from  their governments in part by organizing  under quite difficult quite difficult  circumstances so in the long run so I'm  I'm a China optimist in both in  especially especially in the long run  and I hope that some of that some of the  optimism comes out in the book okay well  this has been fascinating thank you so  much for being here and sharing some of  your work okay thank you for having me  for more information about Professor  Mattingly and his research please visit  our website at macmillan report yale.edu  be sure to join us for another episode  of the Macmillan report made possible  through funding from the Whitney and  Betty Macmillan Center for International  and area studies at Yale  you
 

